WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:10.930
LILLY MCNEALUS: Hi.

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:12.310
My name is Lilly McNealus.

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:14.140
I'm the head of outbound
product management

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.960
and strategy for Vertex
AI at Google Cloud.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.455
Thank you for
joining us at Next.

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:25.330
Today, we're going to talk
about how Vertex AI is now

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:28.870
even easier for developers and
how these advancements help

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:30.790
enterprises to
deploy more models,

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:33.430
gain valuable business
insights, and fully realize

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.190
the value of their
ML investments.

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.300
I'm thrilled that later
in today's session,

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.660
I'll be joined by Matt
Ferrari of Wayfair

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.030
and Jaime Espinoza
of Twitter to discuss

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:46.220
how each of their
organizations have embraced

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.100
Vertex AI to solve
significant challenges

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.110
and evolve their machine
learning strategies.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.510
First, let's set the
scene for the reality

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:58.510
that most organizations
are facing today with AI.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:02.020
Customers tell us, and analysts
confirm, that almost half

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:07.120
of ML models built never
make it into production.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.130
When organizations
aren't able to deploy

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.560
their models their
hardworking teams have built,

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.950
they can't realize the full
value of their AI investments.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.770
This results in low ROI
and a lack of willingness

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:19.840
to continue investing
in AI strategies

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.740
from senior leadership.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.290
This leads to
missed opportunities

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:26.800
to actually use insights from
AI to inform business decision

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.890
and wastes high-value
talent that can actually

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:35.400
lead to retention issues in this
very competitive job market.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.150
So what can organizations do to
increase the number of models

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:42.230
in deployment and actually see a
return on their AI investments?

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:45.810
According to McKinsey's 2020
"The State of AI" report,

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:49.490
AI high performers, though
seeing meaningful return,

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:51.500
have standardized machine
learning platforms

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.830
for all stages of data science,
data engineering, and machine

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:56.390
learning workloads.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:59.360
Organizations who adopt
end-to-end in-house AI

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.640
platforms are more
likely to be successful

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.530
in their implementation
of machine learning

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:05.480
to ease deployment
and to realize

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.580
the value of their
investments more quickly.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.850
So how is Google
helping customers

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:15.600
navigate AI transformations
and avoid common pitfalls?

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.970
Google's commitment to
our customers' success

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.060
means that we are investing
in three focus areas

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.550
to ease ML deployment
in the enterprise.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.240
First, platform centralization
and unification,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.880
bringing together the best
of Google's AI development

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.340
and deployment tools
into a single unified

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.650
workflow for our customers--

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.410
Second, accelerating
time to value--

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.440
our MLOps tooling allows
you to monitor and adjust

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.680
your deployed models faster
and with better insights.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.360
Additionally, we're
bringing to market

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.780
a suite of AI solutions built
to address the most common use

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.020
cases.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.440
And lastly, trust
and responsibility--

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.620
we're continuing to lead in
responsible AI and building

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.490
tools to empower our
customers to do the same.

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:03.660
So let's dig into platform
centralization and unification

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:04.950
a little bit.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.050
Google Cloud offers
a unified ecosystem

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:09.540
of data and AI
capabilities designed

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.150
to make it easy for
everyone to innovate.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.850
We connect the different
types of data processing,

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:19.830
from plan-at-scale OLTP systems
to analytics to data lakes

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.410
to data science to
business analytics.

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.690
We connect the different
roles that work with data

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:27.720
and provide them a common
platform to build upon.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.900
We enable an ecosystem of
external data, partner,

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.680
and open source systems.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:36.150
And finally, we bring all
this together with Vertex AI,

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:38.070
a standardized
end-to-end workflow,

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:41.490
to allow customers to truly
realize the value of their data

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.190
through AI.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.750
You may remember
that at Google I/O

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.220
this year, less than
five months ago,

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:52.050
we announced Vertex AI, which
is a unified AI platform.

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.900
Vertex AI creates the
connections in the data science

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.880
workflow from a
technical standpoint

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.850
to fast-track the
path to ML innovation

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.400
through collaboration,
automation

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.100
of mundane or redundant
tasks, and by integrating

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.390
deployment and management
tools to streamline MLOps.

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.520
The very premise
of Vertex AI was

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.880
to unify Google's ML tool set
and to make experimentation

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.060
process faster,
accelerate deployment,

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:23.530
and to simplify model management
with our MLOps features.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:27.190
We've seen real traction in
customer adoption of Vertex AI.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:30.340
In comparison to previous
versions of our AI platforms

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.470
available for over
five years, we

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:35.560
have seen the following areas
of growth between Vertex AI's

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.300
general availability
being announced

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.890
in May 2021 and August 2021.

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:45.010
The total volume of APIs on
our platform has tripled.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.930
The volume of Vertex
AI training has

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:49.150
increased by 1 and 1/2 times.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.850
And the volume of predictions
on our entire platform

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:55.150
has doubled since Vertex
AI was announced in May.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:59.050
This also has seen-- we've also
seen a 2X increase in GPU hours

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.530
for predictions.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:03.070
We have delivered on our promise
to serve more predictions

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:06.660
and deploy more
models with Vertex AI.

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.720
We know enterprises are often
concerned that increasingly

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.570
centralized workflows could
come at the cost of governance

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.400
and compliance issues.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.990
Google addresses
this by building

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:21.210
Vertex AI with security and
compliance features built in.

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.370
Many of those features,
highlighted in green,

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:26.052
that you see on the slide
were in preview in May.

00:05:26.052 --> 00:05:27.510
And we're excited
to share with you

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.670
that they will be reaching
general availability

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.080
within the coming weeks.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:34.200
We wanted to highlight a
few of these for you today.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.830
Vertex AI Metadata tracks the
inputs and outputs of an ML

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:40.500
pipeline, as well as the
lineage of artifacts,

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:43.980
which can play a critical role
in debugging and compliance.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.630
Once your model
is in production,

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.660
Vertex AI model Monitoring
alerts your data scientists

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:52.140
to data drift for items which
may need their supervision.

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:55.800
This enhances the compliance
and audibility of models.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.260
Vertex AI Pipelines
are reusable pipelines

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:01.830
that help data scientists share
components and iterate quickly.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:05.280
Vertex Pipelines are
built with a Python SDK

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.930
so you can author both KFP
and TFX pipelines with ease.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:11.610
Our Pipelines
service is completely

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.850
serverless, allowing you to
scale up or down as you need

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.980
it-- as you need to
without having to worry

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.110
about managing infrastructure.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:21.990
ML engineers are already
using Vertex AI Pipelines

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:24.630
to move faster from
prototype to production

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:27.830
and to further
accelerate time to value.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:29.960
For more advanced
workloads, enterprises

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:32.960
can now use Vertex AI
Neural Architecture Search

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.790
to optimize neural networks.

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:37.430
And they can use our Vertex
AI Matching Engine service,

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:40.130
which gives the ability to
quickly and efficiently look up

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:43.070
nearest neighbor embeddings
for recommendations and search

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:47.570
need-- without the need
for human intervention.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:49.820
Data science is a
transformational investment

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.700
for many customers
and has continued

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.920
to evolve as AI and
data have become

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:57.710
even more tightly integrated
in the enterprise.

00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:59.720
Customers want
seamless experiences

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.050
across business units and want a
unified data and ML experience.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.230
So today, we're
pleased to announce

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:09.920
a public preview of our
Vertex AI Workbench,

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:12.350
a natural evolution of
Google's Notebook offerings

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:15.530
to respond to the ever-changing
needs of our customers.

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:18.950
Vertex AI Workbench is a single
environment for data scientists

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:21.140
to complete all
of their ML work,

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:24.110
from experimentation to
deployment to managing

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.150
and monitoring models.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.200
It is a Jupyter-based,
fully managed,

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.070
scalable, enterprise-ready
compute infrastructure

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:34.670
with easily enforceable
policies and user management.

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:38.480
With Vertex AI Workbench, data
analysts, data scientists,

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:40.880
and all data and
AI practitioners

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.230
can analyze all of their data,
from BigQuery, Dataproc, Spark,

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:49.220
Looker, and Vertex AI,
all in one interface.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:52.460
Vertex AI Workbench
facilitates data

00:07:52.460 --> 00:07:55.310
to training at scale
with fewer lines of code

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:57.980
and easy connectivity
to our MLOps services

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:01.190
to improve model survivability
at the critical hand-off point

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:02.840
to ML engineers.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.150
Using Vertex AI
Workbench, you can

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:08.330
implement your continuous
integration, training,

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:10.440
and deployment workflows.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.340
Additionally, through our
partnership with NVIDIA,

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:16.910
developers will have the ability
to deploy Vertex AI Workbench

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:20.510
notebooks from NVIDIA
NGC with a single click.

00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:24.630
NVIDIA's NGC is a hub for
GPU-optimized AI software,

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:29.198
including frameworks, models,
SDKs, and Jupyter Notebooks.

00:08:29.198 --> 00:08:30.740
A number of our
customers are already

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:33.620
using Vertex AI Workbench
to ease ML deployment

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.120
and support their
company's transformations.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:38.750
So we're thrilled to include
two of these customers

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:42.860
to share their stories with
us today, Twitter and Wayfair.

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:44.660
Matt and Jaime,
please, could you

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.430
introduce yourselves
and share more

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:50.058
about your organizations
and your ML strategies?

00:08:50.058 --> 00:08:50.850
MATT FERRARI: Sure.

00:08:50.850 --> 00:08:52.020
Well, thank you, Lilly.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:53.850
My name is Matt Ferrari.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:58.830
I lead our MarTech and data
and ML platforms organizations,

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.980
both on the product and
the engineering side.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:05.040
In terms of Wayfair, we're one
of the world's largest home

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:05.670
retailers.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:09.790
We serve just over 31
million active customers.

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:12.173
And we have over 22
million products.

00:09:12.173 --> 00:09:13.590
But often, from
the outside, we're

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:16.500
seen as a retailer or
a home goods company.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:18.670
The truth is we're much more.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:20.880
We have a very
robust platform, one

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:23.070
that scales across
many countries

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:26.850
and is really steeped in
big data, ML, microservices,

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:28.560
and many other
technologies that we're

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.920
going to talk about today,
all in the spirit of bringing

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.940
world-class service
to our customers.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:36.010
LILLY MCNEALUS: Awesome.

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:39.860
And Jaime, can you share
a little about yourself?

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:41.830
JAIME ESPINOSA: Thank you.

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.710
Twitter-- I'm the lead
PM for productizing ML.

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:46.180
My team is called Cortex.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:50.290
It's tasked with accelerating
the development of machine

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:52.070
learning across all of Twitter.

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:54.760
We do this through
research, applied ML,

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:57.220
and we provide an
end-to-end platform

00:09:57.220 --> 00:09:59.860
on which all of the
ML at the company

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:05.590
is created, from ideation all
the way into productization.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:08.402
LILLY MCNEALUS: Great.

00:10:08.402 --> 00:10:10.110
Matt, could you tell
us a little bit more

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:13.380
about your cloud journey and the
partnership with Google Cloud?

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.270
And what were the
key decision factors

00:10:15.270 --> 00:10:19.590
that you contemplated when
making the decision to partner

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.270
with Google Cloud?

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:22.230
MATT FERRARI: Sure.

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:25.800
Wayfair started down the path
with GCP a few years ago,

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:29.670
was an early adopter across
multiple GCP services,

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:33.060
ranging from Kubernetes
to BigQuery to Dataproc.

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:36.570
And we were already partners
with Google for our advertising

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:37.080
business.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.990
But what we really looked
at when we thought about GCP

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:43.230
was an AI-driven
organization that's

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:46.050
also a major player in
the open source community

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:48.010
and a thought leader
in the ML space.

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:52.680
And so we saw Google as
a true technology partner

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.380
and, specifically, were
driven to their openness

00:10:55.380 --> 00:10:59.730
to provide feedback in helping
influence GCP's roadmap.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:02.250
Beyond AI, the
underlying products,

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:05.160
such as BigQuery, Dataproc,
and now Vertex AI,

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:06.120
are examples of that.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:10.920
And it's just the beginning
of Wayfair diving into things

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:13.980
like data velocity, quality,
and data engineering

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.950
as it applies to
machine learning.

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:17.950
LILLY MCNEALUS: Awesome.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:19.408
And Jaime, could
you please tell us

00:11:19.408 --> 00:11:21.940
more about your transformational
priorities for machine

00:11:21.940 --> 00:11:24.790
learning at Twitter and how
GCP is helping you reach

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:27.432
those transformational goals?

00:11:27.432 --> 00:11:28.390
JAIME ESPINOSA: Thanks.

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:30.130
Across Twitter,
there are many teams

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:31.400
that are working together.

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:34.420
And because of the complexity
and the scale at which we're

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:36.790
operating, there are
many opportunities

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:39.100
to leverage machine learning.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:43.390
Our ML platform powers every
stage of the ML project,

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:47.020
from experimentation all
the way into productization

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:50.260
and the maintenance, the MLOps.

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:52.010
It is ultimately
a hybrid system.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:55.660
It's only able to be built
because of the partnership

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:58.710
that we have with GCP.

00:11:58.710 --> 00:11:59.710
LILLY MCNEALUS: Amazing.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:02.375
And Matt, what use cases
or business problems were

00:12:02.375 --> 00:12:04.750
you looking to solve when you
started working with Google

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:08.110
Cloud and adopting Vertex AI?

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:09.010
MATT FERRARI: Sure.

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:11.710
We envisioned a lot of
use cases at Wayfair.

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:14.980
They started in marketing and
search and recommendations.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:17.050
Think about, how can
we make sure you're

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:21.190
searching for the right product
and then recommendations moving

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:21.850
forward?

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:25.600
We thought about product
catalog, supply chain, and even

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:26.920
end-to-end customer service.

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:30.250
And we thought all of that can
benefit from machine learning

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.050
and, in turn, Vertex AI.

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:36.280
In fact, even though it's still
early phases of that journey,

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.650
there's already parts
of that ML lifecycle

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:40.960
that really sing that today.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:44.380
For example, really, we
built it on the principles

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:47.380
of how do we make ML
at Wayfair easier.

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:51.220
That means-- examples would
be how do we accelerate time

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:55.120
to value for new models by
reducing the model training

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.060
duration of each
development iteration.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:01.000
We also thought about
how do we reduce friction

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.730
while we're building and
deploying models at scale

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:07.480
while still increasing
reliability and speed of how

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:10.780
do we retrain jobs to make sure
that data scientists can focus

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:12.790
on data science work.

00:13:12.790 --> 00:13:14.710
And then we really
focused on how do we

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:18.400
enable ML in the
fabric of how we make

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:19.970
decisions for the business.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:23.230
So how do we intertwine it
into our strategic objectives

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:24.340
across Wayfair?

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:26.920
And our early results
have been really positive.

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:30.220
The truth is that we have a lot
of very large model training

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:33.850
jobs that are between 5 and
10 times faster at this point.

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:36.370
And we've gotten feedback
from our data scientists

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:40.270
that they specifically like all
of the hyperparameter tuning

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:41.813
available to them.

00:13:41.813 --> 00:13:43.230
LILLY MCNEALUS:
And on that point,

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:45.720
what is the-- what is most
important to you and your data

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:50.460
scientists when you focus on
deploying ML models at scale?

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:51.510
MATT FERRARI: Sure.

00:13:51.510 --> 00:13:54.180
So I would tell you
that before, it would--

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:57.210
if I interviewed them, they
would say it was really around

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.870
data quality and duplication
of data or lack of integrity

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:01.620
of data--

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:04.950
came out, I don't trust the
data or I have data sprawl

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:05.710
everywhere.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:07.560
And what we really
focused on there

00:14:07.560 --> 00:14:11.280
is how do we drive time to
value for new models and model

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:15.690
enhancements, how do we focus
on quality of predictions that

00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:18.400
drive business impact,
and then, finally,

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:20.730
how do we automate
as much as possible

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:25.020
in a post-deployment model
from a monitoring and tuning

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:26.520
perspective.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:27.520
LILLY MCNEALUS: Awesome.

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:30.040
And I'm curious, Jaime, what
is most important to you

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:32.410
and your data scientists
when you focus on deploying

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:35.960
ML models at scale at Twitter?

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:38.510
JAIME ESPINOSA: Because Twitter
has a unique and incredible

00:14:38.510 --> 00:14:40.460
impact on the world--

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:43.280
maybe because of this fact--
and we are growing very fast.

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:46.070
As Twitter grows, so does
the diversity of the talent

00:14:46.070 --> 00:14:49.370
that that brings in, and so
does the diversity of thought.

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:51.050
The diversity of
techniques and tools

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:52.430
is actually very
important for us

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:56.120
to maintain in order
to be fair as--

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:59.060
and to learn from each other.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:01.820
Twitter offers, also,
a unique opportunity

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:05.390
to work deeply across
multiple ML fields.

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:11.396
This also translates into how
we represent data, how we--

00:15:11.396 --> 00:15:14.200
what kinds of system
requirements we have.

00:15:14.200 --> 00:15:17.660
We have found that
flexibility is really

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:19.130
the key to empower our talent.

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:26.770
I think it's very common
for us, for anybody--

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:29.750
that we are trained with
particular type of tools

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:30.630
and techniques.

00:15:30.630 --> 00:15:32.840
And these are the
things that makes--

00:15:32.840 --> 00:15:35.030
that we're very
familiar with, and is

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:36.800
what makes us the
most productive.

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:41.900
And being able to provide
this within the ML platform,

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:46.190
we have found, is the best
way to enable everybody

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:50.173
to be as productive as possible.

00:15:50.173 --> 00:15:51.090
LILLY MCNEALUS: Great.

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:52.710
And you just mentioned a few.

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.690
But are there any
other key learnings

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:58.050
that you would like to share
with other organizations

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:00.750
that you've come
across as you continue

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:05.210
to scale to the magnitude
you're currently operating at?

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:07.130
JAIME ESPINOSA: And so a--

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:10.760
I'll first give you
a sense for scale.

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:13.520
Twitter operates at
a mind-blowing scale

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:15.650
and a mind-blowing speed.

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:21.590
And these are requirements that
continue to grow very steadily.

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:25.940
I'll share-- over my career,
I've worked in about--

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:29.420
in eight platforms, six of
which were ML platforms.

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:33.050
These were across Intel,
Microsoft, Algorithmia, and now

00:16:33.050 --> 00:16:33.620
Twitter.

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:35.690
And out of all
this experience, I

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:39.440
can honestly say that Twitter
pushes the envelope on almost

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:41.240
every boundary.

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:45.160
And it is based
on that experience

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:49.070
that we've seen
some key learnings.

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:52.820
And this include the
increasing importance

00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:55.820
of the co-design
of the ML models

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:58.730
and the infrastructure,
both of these,

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:01.950
to have a balance between them.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:05.060
So when we think of the
end-to-end platform,

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:08.790
it has to strike a balance that
balances out the infrastructure

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:13.230
or technology with the ML and,
ultimately, with the business.

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:20.420
So one of the biggest predictors
for success for a project

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:25.400
has been how well does
the talent understand both

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:27.900
the ML and the infrastructure.

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.860
And as a-- for
PMs, additionally,

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.110
we also are looking to--

00:17:33.110 --> 00:17:36.470
for understanding of the
business and the product

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:38.330
designs-- so base
product designing them.

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:46.070
Some of these things that
we're trying to address

00:17:46.070 --> 00:17:49.677
are simplified with our
ML platform in which we

00:17:49.677 --> 00:17:52.010
have to track down some of
that infrastructure, where we

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:53.660
make it easier through tools.

00:17:53.660 --> 00:17:56.240
We make it flexible and--

00:17:56.240 --> 00:18:00.500
so that we can better
focus on the ML,

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:03.080
the building of these models.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:04.080
LILLY MCNEALUS: Awesome.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:05.230
That makes a ton of sense.

00:18:05.230 --> 00:18:06.605
And I think that's
exactly how we

00:18:06.605 --> 00:18:09.180
hope folks are thinking
about the advantages

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:12.390
that Vertex AI can bring
to their organizations.

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:14.310
Matt, can you share
a little bit more

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:17.040
about how Vertex AI
helped with your needs

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:19.690
and how easy it
was to get started?

00:18:19.690 --> 00:18:20.850
MATT FERRARI: Sure.

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:24.840
So, so far, to baseline, we've
adopted Vertex AI Workbench

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:29.020
and AI Training components
across the Vertex platform.

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:32.730
Both of those components
have accelerated our adoption

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:34.980
of highly scalable model
development and training

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:38.790
capabilities with a real focus
on are they purpose-built

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:41.910
and at scale for
Wayfair's needs.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:46.200
We've got really enthusiastic
adoption from our own data

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:47.370
science community.

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:50.040
For example, to put
some numbers behind it,

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:53.950
we rolled out Vertex AI
Workbench a few months ago

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:58.260
and, over a period of 30 days,
already had more than 100 users

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:01.230
on the platform spanning
across 35 projects.

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:03.870
And candidly, it's
growing constantly.

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:08.220
In terms of ease, I would say
that we have an ML platform's

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:12.750
organization that specifically
built accelerators for our data

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:15.640
scientists to adopt
these components easily,

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:18.720
such as integrations into
our internal provisioning

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:22.680
solutions, along with
sample code or automatically

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:26.130
created DAGs that
utilize AI training

00:19:26.130 --> 00:19:29.340
platform within
existing Airflow DAGs.

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:32.867
LILLY MCNEALUS: Amazing.

00:19:32.867 --> 00:19:34.700
We're so excited to
hear that you're already

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:38.068
having so much success
with Vertex AI Workbench.

00:19:38.068 --> 00:19:39.860
And at Google, we're
thrilled to contribute

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:42.440
to the transformational ML
journeys of both Twitter

00:19:42.440 --> 00:19:43.430
and Wayfair.

00:19:43.430 --> 00:19:45.470
Thank you both so much
for your time today

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:46.820
and for sharing your insights.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:50.680
And thanks to everyone for
watching and tuning in.

